\"><mrow>\n<mi>y</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mi>p</mi>\n<mi>x</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mn>57</mn>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the equation <math alttext=\"y equals p x plus 57\"><mrow>\n<mi>y</mi>\n<mo>=</mo>\n<mrow>\n<mrow>\n<mi>p</mi>\n<mi>x</mi>\n</mrow>\n<mo>+</mo>\n<mn>57</mn>\n</mrow>\n</mrow>\n</math> correctly expresses <math alttext=\"y\"><mi>y</mi>\n</math> in terms of <math alttext=\"p\"><mi>p</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors.</p>","externalid":"fe86204c-5062-494c-9970-e8162c2a17f3","correct_answer":["B"]},"createDate":1743430554989,"updateDate":1743430554989}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430554989, 1743430554989),
    ($SATQ$da602115$SATQ$, $SATQ$df05bbf9-4751-4ac6-b485-8df58c021387$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a406a6c8-eca4-49dd-b157-b266ff4f2306$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Advanced Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$P$SATQ$, $SATQ$P.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables $SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p>If <math alttext="StartAbsoluteValue 4 x minus 4 EndAbsoluteValue equals 112"><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>112</mn></mrow></math>, what is the positive value of <math alttext="x minus 1"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<mn>1</mn>
</mrow>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["28"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["28"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="28"><mn>28</mn>
</math>. The given absolute value equation can be rewritten as two linear equations: <math alttext="4 x minus 4 equals 112"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>4</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>-</mo>
		<mn>4</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>112</mn>
</mrow>
</math> and <math alttext="minus left parenthesis 4 x minus 4 right parenthesis equals 112"><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>112</mn></math>, or <math alttext="4 x minus 4 equals negative 112"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>4</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>-</mo>
		<mn>4</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mo>-</mo><mn>112</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Adding <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> to both sides of the equation <math alttext="4 x minus 4 equals 112"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>4</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>-</mo>
		<mn>4</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>112</mn>
</mrow>
</math> results in <math alttext="4 x equals 116"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mn>4</mn>
		<mi>x</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>116</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> results in <math alttext="x equals 29"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>29</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Adding <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> to both sides of the equation <math alttext="4 x minus 4 equals negative 112"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mrow>
			<mn>4</mn>
			<mi>x</mi>
		</mrow>
		<mo>-</mo>
		<mn>4</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mo>-</mo><mn>112</mn>
</mrow>
</math> results in <math alttext="4 x equals negative 108"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mn>4</mn>
		<mi>x</mi>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mo>-</mo><mn>108</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> results in <math alttext="x equals negative 27"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mo>-</mo><mn>27</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the two values of <math alttext="x minus 1"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<mn>1</mn>
</mrow>
</math> are <math alttext="29 minus 1"><mn>29</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, or <math alttext="28"><mn>28</mn>
</math>, and <math alttext="negative 27 minus 1"><mo>-</mo><mn>27</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math>, or <math alttext="negative 28"><mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn>
</math>. Thus, the positive value of <math alttext="x minus 1"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<mn>1</mn>
</mrow>
</math> is <math alttext="28"><mn>28</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Alternate approach: The given equation can be rewritten as&nbsp;<math alttext="StartAbsoluteValue 4 left parenthesis x minus 1 right parenthesis EndAbsoluteValue equals 112"><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>112</mn></math>, which is equivalent to&nbsp;<math alttext="4 StartAbsoluteValue x minus 1 EndAbsoluteValue equals 112"><mn>4</mn><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>112</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math alttext="4"><mn>4</mn>
</math> yields&nbsp;<math alttext="StartAbsoluteValue x minus 1 EndAbsoluteValue equals 28"><mfenced open="|" close="|"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn></math>. This equation can be rewritten as two linear equations: <math alttext="x minus 1 equals 28"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mi>x</mi>
		<mo>-</mo>
		<mn>1</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>28</mn>
</mrow>
</math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math alttext="minus left parenthesis x minus 1 right parenthesis equals 28"><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn></math>, or <math alttext="x minus 1 equals negative 28"><mrow>
	<mrow>
		<mi>x</mi>
		<mo>-</mo>
		<mn>1</mn>
	</mrow>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mo>-</mo><mn>28</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the positive value of <math alttext="x minus 1"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>-</mo>
	<mn>1</mn>
</mrow>
</math> is <math alttext="28"><mn>28</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"da602115","external_id":"df05bbf9-4751-4ac6-b485-8df58c021387","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"a406a6c8-eca4-49dd-b157-b266ff4f2306","uId":"e9c7d460-326e-4a56-9d1b-700b343e12f0","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"P","domain":"Advanced Math","skill_cd":"P.B.","skill_desc":"Nonlinear equations in one variable and systems of equations in two variables ","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p>If <math alttext=\"StartAbsoluteValue 4 x minus 4 EndAbsoluteValue equals 112\"><mfenced open=\"|\" close=\"|\"><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>112</mn></mrow></math>, what is the positive value of <math alttext=\"x minus 1\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t<mn>1</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["28"],"correct_answer":["28"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"28\"><mn>28</mn>\n</math>. The given absolute value equation can be rewritten as two linear equations: <math alttext=\"4 x minus 4 equals 112\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>112</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"minus left parenthesis 4 x minus 4 right parenthesis equals 112\"><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>112</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"4 x minus 4 equals negative 112\"><mrow>\n\t<mrow>\n\t\t<mrow>\n\t\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t\t\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t\t</mrow>\n\t\t<mo>-</mo>\n\t\t<mn>4</mn>\n\t</mrow>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mo>-</mo><mn>112</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Adding <math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math> to both sides of the equation <math alttext=\"4 x minus 4 equals 112\"><mrow>\n\t<mr